  "worked_solution": "First, let $f(x)=3-x$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}-6x+9.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 3-x & ={x}^{2}-6x+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=2$ and $x=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=2$ to $x=3.$\n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14261267/assets/b793d15c35fdd668e988459bcfe6c3978eab822c57bfe0fabde463e68e69c04e.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)={x}^{2}-6x+9,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=3-x.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}x([3-x]-[{x}^{2}-6x+9])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}x(-{x}^{2}+5x-6)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}(-{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}-6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3}-3{x}^{2})|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(-\\frac{81}{4}+45-27)-(-4+\\frac{40}{3}-12)] \\\\  & =2\\pi (-\\frac{9}{4}-(-\\frac{8}{3})) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{5}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
